  "style": {
    "id": "pottery-wheel",
    "name": "Pottery Wheel",
    "description": "A potter's wheel in a sunlit plaster studio: wet, glossy grey-brown clay spins on a steel wheel head inside a pale splash pan, a window's light patch on the wall behind it and a shelf of finished glazed pots out of focus. The clay is one lathe surface whose profile is a pure function of t, morphing between named key profiles (lump, cone, opened cylinder, bowl, collar, vase) bottom-up like a real pull, with its volume held constant so it is always the same clay. Spin reads through slip streaks, throwing rings that appear once it is thrown, a lumpy off-centre wobble that damps as it is centred, and marks on the wheel head. A wooden rib on a handle rides the wall exactly where the profile is changing fastest. At the end the wheel slows and a brush sweeps glaze on in a spiral from foot to lip. Wheel hum pitched by spin speed, wet squelches at shape changes and the brush come from the same plan.",
